<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The same two concurrent edits can yield one winning value, two retained alternatives, or a merged structure. Each result can converge; the product decision is which information it may discard.</p>

<!-- @before Comparison Matrix :: guide -->
### Compare the lost information

An LWW register chooses one ordered write and discards another from the current value. An OR-set preserves type-defined membership intent; a counter preserves contributions. None of these choices automatically protects inventory, permissions or money. [The CRDT study](https://www.lip6.fr/Marc.Shapiro/papers/2011/Comprehensive-CRDTs-RR7506-2011-01.pdf) separates convergence conditions from application semantics.

<!-- @before Migration and Reversibility :: staff -->
### Older writers remain part of the protocol

A legacy replacement request can erase the metadata a new merge endpoint needs. Gate capabilities or retire those writers at an explicit reset boundary. Include lost-edit recovery and offline-replica lifetime in the migration decision, rather than treating schema conversion as the entire rollout.

<!-- @before Comparison Matrix :: visual -->
### The same concurrent intent can have different surviving state

```mermaid
flowchart TB
 A["A removes x; replacement set empty; stamp 9"]
 B["B concurrently re-adds x; new tag; stamp 8"]
 L["LWW whole-set register: empty"]
 O["Observed-remove set: x survives"]
 A -->|"ordered replacement"| L
 B -->|"ordered replacement"| L
 A -->|"remove observed tags"| O
 B -->|"retain new tag"| O
```

Starting with x present, A removes the observed membership while B concurrently re-adds x under a new tag. An LWW register holding the entire set can select A’s empty replacement; an observed-remove set preserves B’s unseen add. The chosen stamps and tags are illustrative.
